<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">worldmap</ansi>

In the web client, the Map window draws the rooms you have seen as painted
orbs joined by lines, with where you are marked on them. The settings button
switches to the Tiles style (terrain tiles with your figure walking on them)
or the Classic squares and letters. The text clients have <ansi fg="command">map</ansi> (<ansi fg="command">help map</ansi>)
instead.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Painted orbs</ansi>

  Each room is a round painting of what it is, in a bronze rim. Its ground
  shows through (grass, forest, stone streets, sand, cave rock and the
  rest), and a landmark room shows its building over it: an inn, bank,
  temple, gate, keep or shop. A room with no landmark of its own can take
  one from its name: a castle shows its keep, a library its shelves, a
  vault the bank and a training yard the trainer, and an alley or beggars'
  lane is painted on the slums' cobbles.

  Bronze lines join each room to the rooms its exits lead to. An exit
  somewhere you have not been, or on into another region, fades out a
  short way along. A locked door shows a key on its line and a secret
  passage you have found a question mark. Stairs up or down show a small
  chevron beside the orb.

  A road with no landmark is a smaller orb with its track painted across
  it, running out the way its exits go over the ground it passes through,
  with ruts, stones, tufts of grass and now and then a milestone, so no two
  stretches look alike.

  A room's resource icons sit in a ring round its orb, starting at the
  bottom, each on a dark disc and kept clear of the lines. A picked-clean
  resource has the empty-basket mark. Zoomed out too far to read, they
  are coloured dots.

  No figures stand on the orb map. Your room has a gold rim, and with the
  <ansi fg="yellow">Halo</ansi> on a glowing ring round it glides along as you walk. Your company's
  shield sits at its upper right. A party member shows as a small pennant
  over their room. Your camp is pitched beside its orb, up and to the
  left: the tent with its fire in front, the rough camp, or the inn's bed
  while you rest at an inn.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Terrain (Tiles style)</ansi>

  Each room is a small picture of its ground: grass, forest, road, water,
  shore, swamp, snow, desert, cave, dungeon, city and the rest. Rooms that
  touch share an edge, so a region reads as one stretch of country. Here
  and there water ripples, swamp bubbles, snow falls and sand drifts; a
  setting for reduced motion in your browser keeps them still.

  A road joins the roads beside it that it has an exit to, so it bends,
  forks and crosses as the paths do. Where an exit leads somewhere you
  have not been, or on into another region, the road runs on off the edge.
  A shore follows the water: its sand meets every neighbouring water tile.

  A lake is open water, not a place you can stand. Once you have walked
  all the way round a ring of shore, the lake fills its middle, and the
  shore curves round its corners; you cannot click it or walk onto it.

  Where two rooms touch but have <ansi fg="yellow">no exit between them</ansi>, a dark edge marks the wall,
  so the map never suggests a way through. An exit between rooms that do
  not touch is a line. A locked door shows a key and a secret passage you
  have found shows a question mark. Stairs up and down show a small
  chevron in the tile's corner.

  An exit you have not been through ends in a patch of <ansi fg="yellow">fog</ansi>: the edge of what
  you know. A room whose ground has no picture shows plain stone.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Landmarks (Tiles style)</ansi>

  An inn, bank, trainer, temple, gate, wall, bridge, keep and other
  places drawn from the room's type show a small building or sign over the
  terrain instead of a letter. A room with a symbol of its own but no
  picture keeps its letter, outlined so you can read it. Hover over any
  room for its name and type.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">You (Tiles style)</ansi>

  You are drawn as your class: a warrior, cleric, rogue, ranger, wizard or
  witch. Before you have chosen one, or when a class has no picture of its
  own, you are an adventurer. A gold ring under your feet marks your tile,
  so you never blend into the ground.

  When you move, you walk to the next tile and face the way you went:
  north shows your back, south your front, east and west your side. A
  recall or a teleport jumps without walking.

  Crossing into a new place (another zone, or up or down a level) fades
  the old map to black and brings in the new one under its name for a
  moment, with the level when it isn't the one the place's map starts on:
  "level 2 below" means two levels down. A cave that is a place of its own
  shows its own map when you go in by its mouth, usually dark. With
  reduced motion the name shows without the fade.

  Zooming moves between sizes that keep the pixels sharp, so the picture
  never smears.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Your company</ansi>

  A small shield with a number sits over you: how many of your company are
  with you (you, and each companion who is present and not separated or
  fallen). It is hidden when you travel alone (<ansi fg="command">help company</ansi>).

  In the Tiles style, companions who are with you stand beside you, drawn as their class
  (small, at three quarters of your size) and walking with you. Up to four
  are drawn; the shield still counts everyone. One whose class is not
  known is counted but not drawn.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Your party</ansi>

  In the Tiles style, a party member whose class is known is drawn as that class, a little
  smaller than you and with a small pennant over them. One whose class is
  not known stays a heart. Nobody outside your party appears on the map.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Camps</ansi>

  In the Tiles style, while you stand in your own camp, the camp is you: your figure and your
  companions are round the fire, and the tent with its fire marks where you
  are. Walk off and you step out of it; the camp stays where you pitched it.
  At an inn you keep your figure, and with Sprites off you stay the red
  square, with the tent on it.

  Your camp shows as a tent on its tile (<ansi fg="command">help camp</ansi>). A lit fire flickers beside
  it with smoke rising; an unlit one sits dark. While your company rests,
  a small sleeping mark floats over the tent; resting at an inn shows the
  inn's bed instead. A fire that has burned down to embers glows low with
  no smoke, and a camp made without a tent shows two bedrolls round a ring
  of stones instead of the tent, its one fire burning in that ring
  (<ansi fg="command">help camp gear</ansi>). The camps of your own party show as blue tents, with
  their own fires. You never see the camp of a company that is not in
  your party, even one a step away.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Resources</ansi>

  In the Tiles style, small icons packed inside a tile mark what it provides, such as fresh
  water, forage or a fishing spot (<ansi fg="command">help resources</ansi>). Every resource shows, each
  on a dark disc so it stands out on forest. A resource that has been
  picked clean has an empty-basket mark over its icon, and the mark clears
  on its own when the resource regrows, even in a room you are not standing
  in. On the Classic style, and on a tile zoomed too small for icons, each
  shows as a coloured dot instead.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Walking by clicking</ansi>

  Click another room and pick <ansi fg="yellow">Walk to [room]</ansi> to have your company walk there
  (<ansi fg="command">help walkto</ansi>). Small dots mark the rooms ahead and a flag marks the
  destination until you arrive or stop; the same click offers <ansi fg="yellow">Stop walking</ansi>
  while a walk is under way. The server only walks to rooms you have visited.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Day and night</ansi>

  Outdoor tiles darken and cool as the game's evening comes, with an hour of
  dusk before nightfall and an hour of dawn after sunrise. Rooms under a roof or
  underground are not shaded, as the sky does not reach them.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Light and dark</ansi>

  The map shows what your character can see, as <ansi fg="command">look</ansi> and <ansi fg="command">light</ansi> tell you.
  By day you see everything you have seen. At night, underground, indoors
  with no light, or in fog, sight is shorter. Each light helps one step:
    one light (the moon, a lantern, a torch, a party light or a campfire)
    shows your own room;
    two together (say, moonlight and your lantern), or a lit hall or street,
    show your room and the rooms its exits join, in a soft pool of light;
    in total darkness you can't see even your room, and your figure is faint
    (with sprites off, your room keeps a faint red outline).
  Fog by day draws as a pale veil rather than night.
  Rooms out of sight are remembered: drawn dark and grey, with no people,
  landmarks or resources on them, so you can still find your way back.
  A torch or lantern shows at your hand (a dark lantern when it is out),
  your own party light floats over your head, and a lit campfire glows
  wherever it is. Night vision has no glow; what you see turns a cool grey.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Weather</ansi>

  The map shows the weather you can see (<ansi fg="command">weather</ansi> tells you the same)
  over the outdoor tiles, faintly, so the map stays readable:
    overcast skies drift cloud shadows across the land;
    rain falls diagonally across the tiles, with raindrops splashing;
    a storm is heavier rain under a darker sky, and every few seconds
    lightning strikes, with a brief flash;
    mist, fog and thick fog drift, ever thicker;
    snow falls where a place has snowy weather.
  Weather never falls indoors or underground. Seen from indoors through an
  exit, it falls outside but not in your room; at night or in the dark it
  shows only where you can see, but fog covers everything, as it hides it. With reduced motion the weather stays still
  and lightning strikes without the flash.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Settings</ansi>

  The settings button on the map has <ansi fg="yellow">Style</ansi> (Orbs, the default; Tiles, for
  terrain tiles and figures; or Classic, the plain coloured squares and
  letters, where shape, size and spacing apply), <ansi fg="yellow">Sprites</ansi> (Tiles style
  only: on, or off for the classic red square and hearts), <ansi fg="yellow">Camps</ansi> (on, or
  off to hide every camp marker), <ansi fg="yellow">Day/night</ansi> (on, or off for no shading and
  no darkness), <ansi fg="yellow">Weather</ansi> (on, or off for a clear map) and <ansi fg="yellow">Halo</ansi> (on, or off to
  hide the glowing ring round your orb, or in the Tiles style the ring
  under your sprite and your camp fire; your orb keeps its gold rim). The
  weather shows on the Orbs and Tiles styles (on the orbs it and the night
  fall on the pictures, leaving the ground between them clear). All are
  kept per browser. The map shows only what the game already tells you:
  nothing about anyone outside your party.

See also: <ansi fg="command">help walkto</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help webclient</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help camp</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help resources</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help company</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help party</ansi>.
